<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galdan Boshugtu Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1644">1644</a>–<a href="/years/1697">1697</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Galdan Boshugtu · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choros-oirats">Choros (Oirats)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galdan Boshugtu Khan was ruler of the Dzungar Khanate from 1671 to 1697. A former Buddhist monk trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, he conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> in 1680 and invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha Mongols</a> in 1688. This brought him into war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> of the Qing, who defeated him at Ulan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buton-island">Butung</a> in 1690 and Jao Modo in 1696.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Galdan was the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a>, founder of the Dzungar Khanate, and a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen Taishi</a>; his mother was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a>, the first Khoshut king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. At the age of seven he was sent to be educated as a lama under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> and spent twenty years studying Buddhist texts, philosophy, astronomy, astrology and medicine. When his half-brother Chechen Tayiji murdered his brother Sengge in 1670, Galdan renounced his vows, returned to the Irtysh Valley and defeated his rivals in 1671.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Galdan married Sengge's widow Anu-Dara, a granddaughter of Ochirtu Khan of the Khoshut. In 1678 he drove Ochirtu to Kokonur and established his hegemony over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>, and the following year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> gave him the title Boshoghtu Khan. At the Dalai Lama's request he overthrew the Black Mountain Khoja in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altishahr">Altishahr</a> and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afaq-khoja">Afaq Khoja</a> as his client, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> until 1757. His armies then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauke-khan">Tauke Khan</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> in 1683 and ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayram">Sayram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a> held out.</p><p>Supplied with Russian firearms, Galdan invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> in 1688, against the orders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, and won a three-day battle near Olgoi Lake that August. The defeated Khalkha leaders submitted to the Qing. Meanwhile his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsewang-rabtan">Tsewang Rabtan</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> throne in 1689 and passed intelligence to the Qing. Lured south towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, Galdan was defeated at Ulan Butung, about 350 kilometres north of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1696 Kangxi led one army towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kherlen-river">Kherlen River</a> and sent a second north from the Ordos. On 12 June Galdan ran into the western army near the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuul-river">Tuul River</a> and was routed; his wife Anu was killed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> took 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and 40,000 sheep. Abandoned by most of his followers, he took poison in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> near Khovd in 1697. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsewang-rabtan">Tsewang Rabtan</a> delivered his ashes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1698, where they were scattered on the military parade ground; his children were pardoned. A poem about him underlies the song Black Thunder by the Mongolian band The HU.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galdan Boshugtu Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dzungar pressure grew under <b>Galdan</b> and Tsewang Rabtan and culminated in the invasion of 1723.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Galdan Boshugtu Khan</b> fought the Kangxi Emperor of the Qing until his defeat at Jao Modo in 1696, and after a succession crisis Qing armies conquered the khanate in 1755 and crushed the rising of Amursana by 1757.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1680 the Aqtaghliq leader Afaq Khoja invited the Dzungar Khanate under <b>Galdan Boshugtu</b>, which conquered the khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Black Irtysh. Died in Khovd Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47,92.75&amp;year=1678" aria-label="Khovd Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Galdan Boshugtu Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5160 6099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5160 6099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47,92.75&amp;year=1678">What was here in 1678? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
